%T A401148 108377,108461,142223,156041,175759,175949,191353,202031,207041,
%U A401148 212557,212827,212897,229601,278123,297421,344863,351929,359311,419053,434303,434933,450277
%N A401148 Primes p for which there exists an even number d with p + d < d^2 < p + 2d such that p+d, p+2d and p+3d are also prime.
%C A401148 On the Ulam spiral (1 at the center; ring r >= 1 consists of the numbers (2r-1)^2+1 .. (2r+1)^2), the four cells obtained from one cell of ring r by rotations through 0, 90, 180 and 270 degrees about the center contain the numbers m, m+2r, m+4r, m+6r, where m is the smallest of them, (2r-1)^2 < m < (2r-1)^2 + 2r. This sequence lists the primes m for which all four numbers are prime; with d = 2r this is exactly the condition in the name.
%C A401148 For every p there is at most one even d with p + d < d^2 < p + 2d, since the intervals (d^2-2d, d^2-d) for d = 2, 4, 6, ... are disjoint.
%C A401148 For all terms d is a multiple of 6 (otherwise one of the four numbers is divisible by 3).
%C A401148 The number of terms grows as predicted by the Hardy-Littlewood k-tuple conjecture: there are 417649 terms with p+3d <= 316227^2 = 99999515529 (rings r <= 158113), while the prediction gives about 416450.
%C A401148 Similarly, requiring p+2, p+d+2, p+2d+2, p+3d+2 to be prime as well (four pairs of twin primes on the four rotated cells) gives 22920 cases below 10^15; the first one is p = 110239301.
%H A401148 Jacek Szymkowiak, <a href="/A401148/b401148.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%e A401148 a(1) = 127: d = 12, 127 + 12 = 139 < 144 < 151 = 127 + 24, and 127, 139, 151, 163 are all prime. On the Ulam spiral these are the four cells of ring 6 that are images of each other under rotations through 90 degrees.
%o A401148 (PARI) isok(p) = if(!isprime(p), return(0)); my(s=sqrtint(p)); forstep(d=2*((s-2)\2), s+4, 2, if(d>0 && p+d<d^2 && d^2<p+2*d, return(isprime(p+d) && isprime(p+2*d) && isprime(p+3*d)))); 0;
%o A401148 (Python)
%o A401148 from sympy import isprime
%o A401148 from math import isqrt
%o A401148 def ok(p):
%o A401148     if not isprime(p): return False
%o A401148     s = isqrt(p)
%o A401148     for d in range(2*((s-2)//2), s+5, 2):
%o A401148         if d > 0 and p+d < d*d < p+2*d:
%o A401148             return isprime(p+d) and isprime(p+2*d) and isprime(p+3*d)
%o A401148     return False
%o A401148 print([p for p in range(2, 600000) if ok(p)])
%Y A401148 Cf. A000040.
